WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.416
RETO MEIER: Today,
I learned where

00:00:01.416 --> 00:00:03.940
rats like to hang out when
they're in New York City.

00:00:03.940 --> 00:00:07.170
And which neighborhoods complain
too much, and which too little,

00:00:07.170 --> 00:00:09.280
about seeing furry
rodent visitors.

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:11.160
I'm Reto Meier, Google
Developer Advocate.

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:13.710
Join me as I upload and then
investigate the New York City

00:00:13.710 --> 00:00:15.730
rodent inspection data
set with BigQuery.

00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:23.670
Using the 311 one table from
the New York City public data

00:00:23.670 --> 00:00:26.790
sets in BigQuery, we can see a
dramatic increase in complaints

00:00:26.790 --> 00:00:29.130
about rats starting in 2013.

00:00:29.130 --> 00:00:31.300
If we bucket those
reports into locations,

00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:32.759
we can even create
a heat map that

00:00:32.759 --> 00:00:34.860
shows just the density
of rat sightings reported

00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:36.060
across the city.

00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:38.310
But that only tells
us part of the story.

00:00:38.310 --> 00:00:40.920
To better understand the true
extent of rats in New York,

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:42.750
we need to get more data.

00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:44.580
New York City Open
Data Project includes

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:46.920
this table of written
inspections across the five

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:47.460
boroughs.

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:49.710
We're going to load
that data into BigQuery.

00:00:49.710 --> 00:00:52.694
We'll start by downloading the
CSV file, and while we're here,

00:00:52.694 --> 00:00:55.110
let's grab the data dictionary
and import that into Google

00:00:55.110 --> 00:00:56.460
Sheet so we can use the schema.

00:01:00.070 --> 00:01:02.530
Now we go back to BigQuery.

00:01:02.530 --> 00:01:04.480
Select a data set and
press the plus symbol

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:06.160
to create a new table.

00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:07.990
For small files
up to 10 megs, you

00:01:07.990 --> 00:01:10.030
can upload it here directly.

00:01:10.030 --> 00:01:12.710
But our vermin table
is around 200 megs,

00:01:12.710 --> 00:01:16.180
so instead we'll upload it to
Google Cloud Storage first.

00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:18.070
You can also import
data from Google Drive,

00:01:18.070 --> 00:01:20.620
but we'll save that
for another episode.

00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:23.270
Once we have a location for
our newly uploaded rodent table

00:01:23.270 --> 00:01:26.110
CSV, we'll return to
BigQuery and enter that,

00:01:26.110 --> 00:01:29.260
as well as providing a
name for our new table.

00:01:29.260 --> 00:01:31.150
Then it's just a case
of specifying a schema

00:01:31.150 --> 00:01:33.490
by adding the name and
type of each column.

00:01:33.490 --> 00:01:35.470
We'll use the data
dictionary we imported

00:01:35.470 --> 00:01:38.740
into Google Sheets earlier as
a guide for the column names

00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:41.260
and to get the
corresponding data types.

00:01:41.260 --> 00:01:43.840
We won't need all of these
columns for our analysis,

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:46.090
but there's no cost
for importing data

00:01:46.090 --> 00:01:48.040
so we can happily
pool it all in now

00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:50.320
and then trim our table later.

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:52.660
In this table, it turns
out that the time stamp

00:01:52.660 --> 00:01:56.780
format used in the CSV file
isn't compatible with BigQuery.

00:01:56.780 --> 00:02:00.070
So we'll treat it as a string
for the initial import.

00:02:00.070 --> 00:02:03.820
Once the import job is done,
we can review the new table.

00:02:03.820 --> 00:02:05.860
Let's immediately
use it as a source

00:02:05.860 --> 00:02:07.220
of creating another table.

00:02:07.220 --> 00:02:09.490
This one will include
only the columns we need,

00:02:09.490 --> 00:02:12.820
latitude, longitude, borough
inspection, type, and results.

00:02:12.820 --> 00:02:16.650
And convert the time stamp
strings into real time stamps.

00:02:16.650 --> 00:02:18.630
Once we save the
results to a new table,

00:02:18.630 --> 00:02:21.960
we can delete the CSV file from
Cloud Storage and the larger

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:23.820
original import table.

00:02:23.820 --> 00:02:26.170
BigQuery charges $0.02
per gig, per month,

00:02:26.170 --> 00:02:27.390
pro-rated each second.

00:02:27.390 --> 00:02:30.180
The table we're going
to use is only 63 megs,

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:32.910
so we'd need to keep it
for almost eight months

00:02:32.910 --> 00:02:36.330
before we got charged a
full cent for using it.

00:02:36.330 --> 00:02:39.040
With that in mind, we're all set
to continue our investigation.

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:41.910
We'll start by finding the total
number of inspections conducted

00:02:41.910 --> 00:02:43.050
each year.

00:02:43.050 --> 00:02:44.910
We can see a trend
similar to the 311

00:02:44.910 --> 00:02:48.870
calls over the past three years,
but with an odd bump in 2012.

00:02:48.870 --> 00:02:51.690
One of the advantages
of using this new table

00:02:51.690 --> 00:02:54.460
is that it also includes
inspection results.

00:02:54.460 --> 00:02:58.290
We can see that about 75% of
the time, the initial inspection

00:02:58.290 --> 00:02:59.790
actually passes.

00:02:59.790 --> 00:03:01.590
Now if we limit our
results to inspections

00:03:01.590 --> 00:03:03.180
that find active
rat signs, we can

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:05.160
see that the 2012
bump corresponds

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:09.000
to a significant increase in
inspections, but not in rats.

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:12.870
That's not the case in 2016,
increases across the board.

00:03:12.870 --> 00:03:14.490
But New York is a big city.

00:03:14.490 --> 00:03:16.140
If we examine the
trends per borough,

00:03:16.140 --> 00:03:19.140
looking only to inspections
that found active rat signs,

00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:22.050
we can see that while there
was an uptick across the board,

00:03:22.050 --> 00:03:23.850
Manhattan and Brooklyn
are definitely

00:03:23.850 --> 00:03:27.810
catching up to the Bronx,
crowned rat capital in 2011.

00:03:27.810 --> 00:03:29.280
We can continue
our investigation

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:32.040
by using the locations of
each specific inspection

00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:34.620
to create heat maps telling
which neighborhoods have

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:37.380
the most rat signs and what
the current trends suggest

00:03:37.380 --> 00:03:39.979
for the future of
rats in New York.

00:03:39.979 --> 00:03:42.270
Check out the blog post listed
in the description below

00:03:42.270 --> 00:03:45.620
to see where those
investigations lead.

00:03:45.620 --> 00:03:47.550
BigQuery includes a range
of public data sets,

00:03:47.550 --> 00:03:50.830
including over 130 gigabytes
of New York City data,

00:03:50.830 --> 00:03:53.910
including calls to the 311
municipal complaints line.

00:03:53.910 --> 00:03:56.910
As always, your first byte
of data processing each month

00:03:56.910 --> 00:03:57.790
is free.

00:03:57.790 --> 00:04:00.450
Go to BigQuery today to
import your own data sets

00:04:00.450 --> 00:04:04.470
and share the results with
us using the TILwBQ hashtag.

00:04:04.470 --> 00:04:07.380
And tune in each week to
learn more about BigQuery

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:08.820
with tips and tricks.

00:04:08.820 --> 00:04:10.830
Subscribe to the Google
Cloud YouTube channel

00:04:10.830 --> 00:04:14.410
and follow our blog to learn
something new with BigQuery.

